From a81a682f6b7e678d4b43ed952c8057ec82447661 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 06:20:02 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 152/159] hydra: tie a single surface's accumulated layers for
 the SVM compiler

Houdini's Material Mixer in its single-surface mode writes each input of
one standard_surface as a chain of adds, acc_k = acc_(k-1) + (layer_k -
acc_(k-1)) x w_k. Nothing told the SVM compiler the layers apart: it
evaluated every layer's tiled lookups before the first add, and the
ten-layer Gauge terrain overflowed the 255 stack slots and rendered black,
where the same mixer's mix_surfaceshader form had been folded and tied
layer by layer (K-110).

TieSumChains walks the add chains behind the surface's inputs (through a
normalize for the normal) and ties each layer's own geometry lookups to
the previous accumulation. The ties FoldSurfaceMix used are lifted into
LayerTies, shared by both. Ten layers: 172 slots, and the sphere at 0.068
against Karma's 0.069 (K-115).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
